existingSections := splitBody(existingBody)\n\tincomingPreamble, incomingSections := splitBody(incomingBody)\n\n\t// map from incoming heading NAME -> incoming section\n\tincomingByName := make(map[string]bodySection, len(incomingSections))\n\tincomingOrder := make([]string, 0, len(incomingSections))\n\tfor _, sec := range incomingSections {\n\t\tname, _ := headingName(sec.heading) // it's a heading, so ok is true\n\t\tif _, seen := incomingByName[name]; !seen {\n\t\t\tincomingOrder = append(incomingOrder, name)\n\t\t}\n\t\tincomingByName[name] = sec\n\t}\n\n\t// preamble: incoming wins if it has any non-whitespace text\n\tpreamble := existingPreamble\n\tif strings.TrimSpace(incomingPreamble) != \"\" {\n\t\tpreamble = incomingPreamble\n\t}\n\n\t// track which incoming headings were used\n\tused := make(map[string]bool, len(incomingByName))\n\n\tvar out strings.Builder\n\tout.WriteString(preamble)\n\texistingNames := make(map[string]bool, len(existingSections))\n\tfor _, sec := range existingSections {\n\t\tname, _ := headingName(sec.heading)\n\t\texistingNames[name] = true\n\t\tif incoming, ok := incomingByName[name]; ok {\n\t\t\tout.WriteString(incoming.heading)\n\t\t\tout.WriteString(incoming.content)\n\t\t\tused[name] = true\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tout.WriteString(sec.heading)\n\t\t\tout.WriteString(sec.content)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\t// append incoming-only sections in incoming order\n\tfor _, name := range incomingOrder {\n\t\tif !existingNames[name] {\n\t\t\tsec := incomingByName[name]\n\t\t\tout.WriteString(sec.heading)\n\t\t\tout.WriteString(sec.content)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn out.String()\n}\n```\n\nWait — but there's an important subtlety. The spec says: \"(5) Append, after all emitted sections and in incoming order, every incoming section whose heading name was NOT present among the existing sections.\" So an incoming section whose name IS among existing sections was already emitted in-place in step 4, and should NOT be appended again. So the `used` map is actually not needed — the condition is `!existingNames[name]`. Right, I'll use that. `used` not needed. Let me drop it.\n\nBut wait: what if there are duplicate heading names within the existing body? E.g. two `## Result` sections on disk? Then `existingNames[\"## Result\"] = true`, and both existing sections iterate: for each, `incomingByName[\"## Result\"]` exists → emit incoming section twice (in place of both). Hmm, that's an edge case not in the tests. The spec doesn't address it. I'll leave it — matches \"for each existing section: if incoming has same name, emit incoming\".\n\nActually wait, there's a subtlety about what \"the incoming map holds a section with the same heading name\" — for step 4 we emit the incoming section in place of the existing section with the same name. If existing has two `## Result` sections and incoming has one, we'd emit incoming's `## Result` twice. That's a degenerate case; the spec algorithm literally says that. Fine.\n\nNow, there's also the preamble concatenation concern: \"Return `resultPreamble` + the concatenated sections.\" What if the on-disk body's preamble is empty but the body starts with a heading? E.g. on-disk body is \"## Result\\nOld content\\n\". Then existingPreamble = \"\", existingSections = [## Result / Old content]. Incoming body \"## Result\\nNew content\\n\" → incomingPreamble \"\", incomingSections [## Result / New content]. Result: preamble \"\" + emitted incoming ## Result section = \"## Result\\nNew content\\n\". Good — matches the same-named-heading-replaced test (AC 7).\n\nNow for AC 8 (on-disk preamble survives when incoming starts with a heading): on-disk body \"Tags: [[Task]]\\n\\n---\\n\\ndescription\\n\\n## Details\\n- x\\n\". Wait, let me parse this. The writeTaskFile content is:\n\n```\n---\\ntask_identifier: ...\\nstatus: in_progress\\nphase: ai_review\\n---\\nTags: [[Task]]\\n\\n---\\n\\ndescription\\n\\n## Details\\n- x\\n\n```\n\nSo after the closing `---\\n` frontmatter delimiter, the body is:\n```\nTags: [[Task]]\n\n---\n\ndescription\n\n## Details\n- x\n```\n(as bytes: \"Tags: [[Task]]\\n\\n---\\n\\ndescription\\n\\n## Details\\n- x\\n\")\n\nsplitBody on this: preamble = \"Tags: [[Task]]\\n\\n---\\n\\ndescription\\n\\n\" (everything before \"## Details\"). sections = [{heading: \"## Details\\n\", content: \"- x\\n\"}].\n\nIncoming body: \"## Result\\nStatus: failed\\n\" → preamble \"\", sections [{## Result\\n, Status: failed\\n}].\n\nmergeBody: incomingPreamble = \"\" (TrimSpace(\"\")==\"\" → use existingPreamble). Result: preamble \"Tags: [[Task]]\\n\\n---\\n\\ndescription\\n\\n\" + existing section \"## Details\\n- x\\n\" (no incoming match) + append incoming-only \"## Result\\nStatus: failed\\n\".\n\nResult body:\n```\nTags: [[Task]]\n\n---\n\ndescription\n\n## Details\n- x\n## Result\nStatus: failed\n```\n\nThe test asserts: ContainsSubstring(\"Tags: [[Task]]\"), ContainsSubstring(\"description\"), ContainsSubstring(\"## Result\"). All pass. And the bare `---` line survives verbatim. \n\nNow — important: the `---` line must NOT be treated as a heading. It's \"---\\n\", prefix check \"## \" fails. Good.\n\nBut wait, there's a subtlety in `ExtractBody`. The on-disk file frontmatter is closed with `\\n---\\n`. The body extraction finds `\\n---\\n` — hmm, but the body contains \"---\" lines. Let me re-check ExtractBody: it does `strings.Cut(rest, \"\\n---\\n\")` and takes everything after the FIRST occurrence. So for the AC-8 fixture, after the frontmatter's closing `---`, the body starts with \"Tags:...\". The first `\\n---\\n` in `rest` is the closing delimiter (rest starts with frontmatter content then `\\n---\\n`). Actually wait: `rest = strings.TrimPrefix(s, delim)` where s = \"---\\n...frontmatter...\\n---\\nTags:...\". So rest = \"\\n...frontmatter...\\n---\\nTags...\". Cut on \"\\n---\\n\": first occurrence. Is there a `\\n---\\n` inside the frontmatter? The frontmatter is \"task_identifier: test-task-uuid-1234\\nstatus: in_progress\\nphase: ai_review\" — no \"---\" inside. So first \"\\n---\\n\" is the closing delimiter. after = \"Tags: [[Task]]\\n\\n---\\n\\ndescription\\n\\n## Details\\n- x\\n\". Good, that's the full body including the interior \"---\" line.\n\nNow AC 9 (heading-less both sides): on-disk body \"First content\\n\" → preamble \"First content\\n\", no sections. Incoming \"Second result\\n\" → preamble \"Second result\\n\", no sections. incomingPreamble has non-whitespace → use incoming. Result: \"Second result\\n\". Test asserts ContainsSubstring(\"Second result\\n\") and NotTo ContainSubstring(\"First content\"). \n\nNow CRLF test (AC 10): on-disk body \"## Parked\\r\\n\\r\\nOperator park reason.\\r\\n\\r\\n## Result\\r\\nStatus: failed\\r\\n\".\n\nLet me parse: body = \"## Parked\\r\\n\\r\\nOperator park reason.\\r\\n\\r\\n## Result\\r\\nStatus: failed\\r\\n\"\n\nsplitBody lines:\n- \"## Parked\\r\\n\" → heading name \"## Parked\" (trim \\r\\n). section1 heading=\"## Parked\\r\\n\".\n- \"\\r\\n\" → content of section1 = \"\\r\\n\"\n- \"Operator park reason.\\r\\n\" → content += \"Operator park reason.\\r\\n\"\n- \"\\r\\n\" → content += \"\\r\\n\"\n- \"## Result\\r\\n\" → heading \"## Result\". section2 heading=\"## Result\\r\\n\".\n- \"Status: failed\\r\\n\" → content section2 = \"Status: failed\\r\\n\"\n\nincoming body \"## Result\\nStatus: failed\\n\" → sections [## Result / Status: failed\\n], preamble \"\".\n\nmergeBody: preamble = \"\" (incoming empty). Existing sections iterated:\n- \"## Parked\" → not in incoming → emit verbatim: \"## Parked\\r\\n\" + \"\\r\\nOperator park reason.\\r\\n\\r\\n\"\n- \"## Result\" → in incoming → emit incoming: \"## Result\\nStatus: failed\\n\"\nAppend incoming-only: none (## Result was present).\nResult: \"## Parked\\r\\n\\r\\nOperator park reason.\\r\\n\\r\\n## Result\\nStatus: failed\\n\"\n\nTest asserts: ContainsSubstring(\"## Parked\") ✓, ContainsSubstring(\"Operator park reason.\") ✓, ContainsSubstring(\"## Result\") ✓. \n\nNow, wait — there's a concern with `ExtractBody` for the CRLF fixture. The on-disk file is:\n\"---\\ntask_identifier: test-task-uuid-1234\\nstatus: in_progress\\nphase: ai_review\\n---\\n## Parked\\r\\n\\r\\nOperator park reason.\\r\\n\\r\\n## Result\\r\\nStatus: failed\\r\\n\"\n\nrest after TrimPrefix(\"---\") = \"\\ntask_identifier...\\n---\\n## Parked\\r\\n...\". Cut on \"\\n---\\n\" → after = \"## Parked\\r\\n\\r\\nOperator park reason.\\r\\n\\r\\n## Result\\r\\nStatus: failed\\r\\n\". Good — the CRLF inside body doesn't contain \"\\n---\\n\" so no premature cut. \n\nNow let me think about the `containsEscalationSection` dedup concern for the existing tests. In the already-parked-task spec (carve-out 1), the on-disk body is existingEscalationBody which is:\n\"\\n## Trigger Cap Escalation\\n\\n- **Timestamp:** 2026-04-18T11:00:00Z\\n- **Trigger count:** 3\\n- **Max triggers:** 3\\n- **Assignee:** claude\\n- **Last agent output:** see `## Result` above\\n\"\n\nIncoming content: \"## Result\\nStatus: failed\\n\" + existingEscalationBody = \"## Result\\nStatus: failed\\n\\n## Trigger Cap Escalation\\n\\n...\\n\"\n\nmergeBody(existingBody=\"\\n## Trigger Cap Escalation\\n\\n...\\n\", incomingBody=\"## Result\\nStatus: failed\\n\\n## Trigger Cap Escalation\\n\\n...\\n\")\n\nsplitBody(existing): body starts with \"\\n\" — the first line is \"\\n\" (preamble = \"\\n\"), then \"## Trigger Cap Escalation\\n\" → section1 heading. content = \"\\n- **Timestamp:** ...\\n...\" up to end. So existingSections = [## Trigger Cap Escalation]. existingPreamble = \"\\n\".\n\nsplitBody(incoming): \"## Result\\n\" → section1, \"Status: failed\\n\" content, \"\\n\" content, \"## Trigger Cap Escalation\\n\" → section2, content rest. incomingPreamble = \"\".\n\nmergeBody: preamble = existingPreamble \"\\n\" (incoming preamble empty). \nIterate existing sections:\n- \"## Trigger Cap Escalation\" → in incoming map → emit INCOMING section: heading \"## Trigger Cap Escalation\\n\" + incoming content \"\\n- **Timestamp:** 2026-04-18T11:00:00Z\\n...\" \nAppend incoming-only: \"## Result\" not in existing → append \"## Result\\nStatus: failed\\n\\n\"... wait the incoming ## Result section's content is \"Status: failed\\n\\n\" (up to next heading). \n\nSo result body = \"\\n\" + \"## Trigger Cap Escalation\\n\\n- **Timestamp:** ...\\n\" + \"## Result\\nStatus: failed\\n\\n\"\n\nHmm interesting — so the order is: preamble, then Trigger Cap Escalation (in place of on-disk), then appended ## Result at the end.\n\nWait, that's odd. The on-disk had only the escalation section; the incoming has Result + escalation. So the merged body = \"\\n\" + (incoming Trigger Cap Escalation section) + (incoming Result section appended).\n\nThen applyRetryCounter(merged, currentOnDisk, mergedBody):\n- isTerminalStatus? merged.Status() — status in_progress, not terminal.\n- applyTriggerCap: merged has max_triggers (3), triggerCount = 3 (on-disk, since operator/controller-owned), not < max. agentName := clearAssignee(merged) — merged assignee... hold on.\n\nLet me trace the frontmatter merge for carve-out 1:\n- On-disk FM: status: in_progress, phase: ai_review, trigger_count: 3, max_triggers: 3, assignee: \"\", previous_assignee: claude.\n- Incoming FM: task_identifier, status: in_progress, phase: planning (stale), trigger_count: 3, max_triggers: 3, assignee: claude (stale).\n\nMergeFrontmatter:\n1. Base merge: existing keys + incoming keys. So merged: task_identifier, status: in_progress, phase: planning (incoming), trigger_count: 3, max_triggers: 3, assignee: claude (incoming), previous_assignee: claude (existing).\n2. Controller-owned loop: trigger_count on disk=3, incoming=3, equal → merged[trigger_count]=3, no decision. retry_count: not on disk, not incoming → skip.\n3. Terminal status pin: in_progress not terminal → skip.\n4. NEW operator-owned loop:\n   - field \"assignee\": diskValue = \"\" (on disk, present as assignee: \"\"), incomingValue = \"claude\". onDisk true. inIncoming && field==\"assignee\" && incomingValue==\"\"? No (incoming \"claude\"). merged[\"assignee\"] = diskValue (\"\"). inIncoming && !frontmatterValueEqual(\"\", \"claude\") → decision {Field: \"assignee\", Kept: \"\", Rejected: \"claude\"}.\n   - field \"previous_assignee\": diskValue = \"claude\", incomingValue absent (not in incoming FM). onDisk true. inIncoming false. merged[\"previous_assignee\"] = \"claude\". No decision.\n\nSo merged FM: assignee: \"\", previous_assignee: claude, phase: planning, trigger_count: 3, max_triggers: 3.\n\nThen applyTriggerCap: triggerCount = merged.TriggerCount() = 3, max_triggers present = 3, 3 >= 3, not 0. agentName := clearAssignee(merged) → merged.Assignee() = \"\" → agentName = \"\", skip previous_assignee write, merged[\"assignee\"] = \"\" (already \"\"). \n\nThen `if containsEscalationSection(body, \"## Trigger Cap Escalation\")` — body is mergedBody. Does mergedBody contain \"\\n## Trigger Cap Escalation\\n\"? mergedBody = \"\\n\" + \"## Trigger Cap Escalation\\n\\n...\" + \"## Result\\nStatus: failed\\n\\n\". Yes! It contains \"\\n## Trigger Cap Escalation\\n\". So → restoreExistingPhase(existing, merged) → existing.Phase() = ai_review → merged[\"phase\"] = \"ai_review\". Return body (no duplicate escalation).\n\nThen ClearAssigneeIfHumanReview(merged): phase = \"ai_review\" → no clear.\n\nmerged.SpawnNotification()? Not present → false.\n\nretryCount := merged.RetryCount() = 0 (absent). applyRetryCap: no max_retries → return.\n\nFinal body = mergedBody. Frontmatter marshaled: assignee: \"\", previous_assignee: claude, phase: ai_review, trigger_count: 3, max_triggers: 3.\n\nTest assertions:\n- phase: ai_review present ✓ (restored), NotTo phase: planning ✓\n- NotTo ContainSubstring(\"\\nassignee: claude\") — the frontmatter assignee is \"\" so marshaled as `assignee: \"\"`. Does \"\\nassignee: claude\" appear anywhere? The body has \"- **Assignee:** claude\" (capital A), not \"\\nassignee: claude\". ✓\n- strings.Count(s, \"## Trigger Cap Escalation\") == 1 — body has exactly one. ✓\n- ContainSubstring(\"previous_assignee: claude\") — from frontmatter. ✓ (Carve-out 1 added it to disk; merge preserves it since incoming doesn't have previous_assignee and the disk value wins.)\n\nGreat, this all works with the guard keeping on-disk assignee \"\".\n\nWait — but I need to double check: the decision produced is {Field: \"assignee\", Kept: \"\", Rejected: \"claude\"} → logs `ownership guard kept on-disk: task ... field assignee kept  rejected claude`. That's fine (test doesn't assert on logs).\n\nNow the other existing test at line 939 (\"preserves phase and keeps assignee cleared when deliverer published needs_input\"): \n- On-disk FM: assignee: claude (present), status: in_progress, phase: ai_review.\n- Incoming FM: status: in_progress, phase: ai_review, assignee: \"\".\n\nMergeFrontmatter:\n- Base merge: assignee: \"\" (incoming).\n- Operator-owned: field \"assignee\": onDisk=true (disk \"claude\"), incoming=\"\" and field==\"assignee\" → merged[\"assignee\"] = \"\" (empty-clear exception applied, no decision). ✓\n- previous_assignee: not on disk → skip (continue). ✓\n\nSo merged assignee: \"\". Test asserts:\n- phase: ai_review present ✓\n- NotTo phase: human_review ✓\n- NotTo \"\\nassignee: claude\" ✓ (assignee: \"\")\n- NotTo \"## Retry Escalation\" ✓\n- NotTo \"## Trigger Cap Escalation\" ✓\n- NotTo \"previous_assignee:\" ✓ — merged has no previous_assignee (disk doesn't have it, incoming doesn't). ✓\n\nBody: on-disk \"Original body\\n\" (no headings) → preamble \"Original body\\n\". Incoming \"Needs human input.\\n\" → preamble. incomingPreamble non-whitespace → result = \"Needs human input.\\n\". applyRetryCounter: not terminal, no caps → returns body. Test doesn't assert on body content for this one. ✓\n\nNow the \"fully replaces content on second call\" test (line 221): \n- On-disk after first write: body \"First result\\n\". Wait, the first write's on-disk body was \"First content\\n\", incoming \"